<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Meerssen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/870">870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Meerssen was concluded in August 870 by the Carolingian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, who divided between them the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of Lotharingia after the death of their nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. Louis gained the eastern part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and Charles the western part. The division was largely revised in Louis's line's favour by the Treaty of Ribemont in 880.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen.jpg" alt="Treaty of Meerssen" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vertrag_von_Meerssen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
